libmatroska: a block is a binary element, and no larger than one

KaxInternalBlock is an EbmlBinary, and EbmlBinary::SizeIsValid allows less than 0x7FFFFFFF bytes because a binary element's ReadData allocates its size and reads it in. KaxInternalBlock's own SizeIsValid replaced that with `size >= 4` and nothing above it, while its ReadData still hands the whole block to EbmlBinary::ReadData: so FindNextElement accepts a SimpleBlock or a Block stating any size an EBML number holds, and reading it asks malloc for all of it. MediaPerch's Matroska fuzzer met a 960-byte file with a SimpleBlock stating 0x28100f00096 bytes, 2.7 TiB, which AddressSanitizer stops as larger than any allocation it makes; a build without it asks the system for the terabytes and throws when refused. The block keeps its least of four bytes and takes the binary element's most, so a size no binary element may have is no block either, and FindNextElement goes on looking past it as it does past any other. Against release-1.7.0-289-g67166f0.

diff --git a/matroska/KaxBlock.h b/matroska/KaxBlock.h
index 99e24f3..9fe0e16 100644
--- a/matroska/KaxBlock.h
+++ b/matroska/KaxBlock.h
@@ -186,7 +186,9 @@ class MATROSKA_DLL_API KaxInternalBlock : public libebml::EbmlBinary {
     ~KaxInternalBlock() override;
     bool SizeIsValid(std::uint64_t size) const override
     {
-      return size >= 4; /// for the moment
+      // The track number, the timestamp and the flags at least, and no more
+      // than any binary element: ReadData allocates the whole size.
+      return size >= 4 && EbmlBinary::SizeIsValid(size);
     }
 
     std::uint16_t TrackNum() const {return TrackNumber;}
